1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:21,000
 [ Music ]

2
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,360
 >> There were lots of campus Ethernet's

3
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:25,160
 because they were really easy to deploy

4
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:27,760
 and you could put them in a department

5
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:30,720
 and then you could run a wire between two departments

6
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:35,400
 and you had a bigger network and so we've grown up networks sort

7
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:41,680
 of by agglomeration in lots of different university campuses

8
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:48,840
 and NSF came up with some money, said, oh, we've got a little bit

9
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:51,960
 in our budget where we could get some 56 kilobit lines

10
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:57,200
 and tie those campuses together and they did that,

11
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:00,000
 made the NSF met phase one.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,120
 But now you're tying together 10 megabit campus infrastructure

13
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:13,040
 with 56 kilobit wires and it was wildly popular because people

14
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:15,120
 that couldn't talk could suddenly talk

15
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:19,440
 and they're sending emails and moving huge files

16
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:23,880
 and everybody's really excited about this technology.

17
00:01:23,880 --> 00:01:32,000
 But any one of those campuses could oversubscribe the net

18
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,000
 by a factor of a thousand.

19
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,640
 So we had a lot of packets piling up and getting dropped.

20
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:44,560
 At the time I was a researcher at Lawrence Berkeley Lab,

21
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:48,200
 which is in the hills up above the Berkeley campus

22
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:53,440
 and I was also teaching on the Berkeley campus.

23
00:01:53,440 --> 00:02:01,440
 Even back in those days, which was mid '80s, we had a,

24
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:04,880
 for every class there was a messages group, you know,

25
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:06,560
 like a little news group that was set up.

26
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:10,960
 All the assignments would be put online and I was trying

27
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:16,520
 to get course materials from my office in LBL down to a machine

28
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:23,040
 in Evans Hall at Berkeley and there was like zero throughput

29
00:02:23,040 --> 00:02:27,480
 in the net, it was one packet every 10 minutes or so

30
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:35,240
 and it seemed unbelievably bad and I went down and talked

31
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:40,840
 to Mike Carroll who was heading the BSD group,

32
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:43,360
 the people that developed Berkeley Unix

33
00:02:43,360 --> 00:02:48,560
 and he's getting reports of these problems from all

34
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:52,120
 over the country from, in those days,

35
00:02:52,120 --> 00:02:54,880
 the easiest way to start running TCP/IP was to bring

36
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:59,960
 up Berkeley Unix because there was a ARPA funded very nice

37
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:04,920
 implementation in it and everybody was seeing poor

38
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:11,960
 performance so we talked for a long time that day

39
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:15,160
 and on succeeding days about, well, what's going wrong,

40
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:18,320
 is there some mistake in the protocol implementation,

41
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:21,840
 is there some mistake in the protocol,

42
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:27,000
 this thing was working on smaller scale tests

43
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:28,880
 and then it suddenly fell apart.

44
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:35,520
 I think we struggled for three or four months just going

45
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:40,920
 through the code, writing tools to capture packet traces

46
00:03:40,920 --> 00:03:43,320
 and looking at the packet traces and trying

47
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:50,520
 to sort out what was breaking and I remember the two

48
00:03:50,520 --> 00:03:54,800
 of us were sitting in Mike's office after we'd been pounding

49
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:58,440
 our head against the wall for literally months and one

50
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:01,440
 of us, I can't remember which one, said, "You know,

51
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:03,760
 the reason I can't figure out why it's breaking is

52
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:06,920
 I don't understand how it ever worked."

53
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:11,560
 You know, we're sending these bits out at 10 megabits,

54
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:14,440
 they're zipping across campus, they're running

55
00:04:14,440 --> 00:04:18,840
 into this 56 kilobit wire, we expect them to go

56
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:21,600
 through that wire, pop out on the other side, go through.

57
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:25,280
 How could that function?

58
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:30,000
 That turned out to be the crucial starting point.

59
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,080
 At that point, we started saying, "Well, what is there

60
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:39,160
 about this protocol that makes it work?

61
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:42,360
 How does it deal with all of those bandwidth changes?

62
00:04:42,360 --> 00:04:45,320
 How does it deal with the multiple hops?"

63
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:50,600
 So this picture, that direction is time,

64
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:52,120
 this direction is bandwidth.

65
00:04:52,120 --> 00:04:55,040
 So that's a fat pipe and that's a skinny pipe.

66
00:04:55,040 --> 00:04:58,760
 And the scale of the time is, you know,

67
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:02,520
 this is a 10 megabit pipe and this is a 56 kilobit pipe.

68
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:06,920
 So here the difference is about 3 to 1,

69
00:05:06,920 --> 00:05:10,440
 it was really closer to 100 to 1.

70
00:05:10,440 --> 00:05:17,200
 And time, seconds times bits per second equals bits.

71
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:23,000
 So each of these little boxes in there is a packet,

72
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,080
 it's the number of bits in a packet.

73
00:05:25,080 --> 00:05:29,600
 And if you scrunch it down in bandwidth, it's got to spread

74
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:32,960
 out in time because the number of bits doesn't change.

75
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:38,840
 And so see the burst of packets,

76
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:41,960
 the window's worth of packets gets launched, it's going to fly

77
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:45,960
 through the net until it hits this fast to slow transition.

78
00:05:45,960 --> 00:05:49,760
 And then because the packets have to stretch out in time,

79
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:52,440
 they all have to sit there and wait as they're fed

80
00:05:52,440 --> 00:05:54,280
 into the slower wire.

81
00:05:54,280 --> 00:06:02,320
 And you, they pop out the other side, they get spread

82
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:05,480
 out by this bottleneck, by this slower wire.

83
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:08,440
 Once they're spread out, they stay spread out, right?

84
00:06:08,440 --> 00:06:11,720
 There's nothing to push them back together again.

85
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:16,840
 They hit a receiver, it turns every data packet into an ACK.

86
00:06:16,840 --> 00:06:18,040
 So you've got a bunch of ACKs

87
00:06:18,040 --> 00:06:20,000
 that are going back towards the sender

88
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:22,760
 and they remember what's the right spacing

89
00:06:22,760 --> 00:06:24,920
 for that bottleneck.

90
00:06:24,920 --> 00:06:29,320
 So the ACKs get back to the sender

91
00:06:29,320 --> 00:06:31,840
 and every ACK gets turned into a data packet.

92
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:34,960
 So we can see the data packets flowing back.

93
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:39,400
 And this is after one round trip time.

94
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:43,600
 Now the packets are coming out perfectly spaced so they go by,

95
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:48,720
 a new one goes into the net in exactly the time it takes a

96
00:06:48,720 --> 00:06:51,680
 packet to exit from the bottleneck.

97
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:54,800
 So these ACKs are sort of acting as a clock

98
00:06:54,800 --> 00:06:58,120
 that tells you, tells the sender when it's safe

99
00:06:58,120 --> 00:07:00,080
 to inject every new packet.

100
00:07:00,080 --> 00:07:02,160
 And they're always going to be spaced

101
00:07:02,160 --> 00:07:04,200
 by whatever is the slowest point in the net.

102
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:06,480
 >> And the key thing is, is how do you, how quick,

103
00:07:06,480 --> 00:07:08,000
 how can you get the steady state sort

104
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:09,600
 of most quickly without wasting?

105
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:14,880
 >> Yeah. And the issue, the failure we saw was,

106
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:19,320
 this works perfectly after you've exchanged a round trip

107
00:07:19,320 --> 00:07:21,440
 time worth of packets.

108
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:26,200
 But when you're starting up, when you're here,

109
00:07:26,200 --> 00:07:27,880
 there's no clock.

110
00:07:27,880 --> 00:07:31,560
 And so the hard part on TCP is not getting it running,

111
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:34,760
 it's getting it started because once you've got it running,

112
00:07:34,760 --> 00:07:37,520
 you've got a clock that tells you exactly what to do.

113
00:07:37,520 --> 00:07:40,080
 So if you turn them on, suddenly you get

114
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:42,400
 in this repetitive failure mode

115
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:45,920
 where you saturate the buffering that was available

116
00:07:45,920 --> 00:07:48,440
 at some gateway and then when you retransmit,

117
00:07:48,440 --> 00:07:49,560
 you do the same thing again.

118
00:07:49,560 --> 00:07:51,640
 So you're always losing packets.

119
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:54,800
 But if you turned it on more gradually,

120
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:57,440
 then you wouldn't overload the buffering and you'd get enough

121
00:07:57,440 --> 00:08:01,360
 of a clock going so that you control the amount

122
00:08:01,360 --> 00:08:05,080
 of backlog to fit the available buffer

123
00:08:05,080 --> 00:08:07,520
 but you'd still be growing the number of packets in flight

124
00:08:07,520 --> 00:08:11,120
 so that you'd eventually get a, you'd start with a kind

125
00:08:11,120 --> 00:08:13,760
 of sporadic clock but you'd eventually fill in the details

126
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:15,720
 and get a per packet clock.

127
00:08:15,720 --> 00:08:17,920
 >> How did you get it to the point where it was

128
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:20,640
 in all the TCP/IP implementations on the planet?

129
00:08:20,640 --> 00:08:22,960
 Because they kind of have to cooperate in a way.

130
00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:25,880
 >> So remember it was a much simpler time when you're talking

131
00:08:25,880 --> 00:08:30,040
 about all the TCP/IP implementations on the planet.

132
00:08:30,040 --> 00:08:32,360
 At that time there were like four.

133
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:34,480
 So there was the Berkeley Unix one.

134
00:08:34,480 --> 00:08:36,520
 There was the MIT PCTCP.

135
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:41,440
 There was a BBN one that was used in butterflies and imps

136
00:08:41,440 --> 00:08:44,520
 and there was a Multics one.

137
00:08:44,520 --> 00:08:51,720
 I took the couple of TCP kernel modules that we'd been working

138
00:08:51,720 --> 00:08:53,880
 on, packaged them up in a tar.

139
00:08:53,880 --> 00:08:58,560
 I had this horrible driver hack that would let us snarf packets

140
00:08:58,560 --> 00:09:02,400
 from the kernel, I mean it was really a horrible driver hack.

141
00:09:02,400 --> 00:09:06,160
 It was the way you said what you wanted to snarf was by ADB

142
00:09:06,160 --> 00:09:11,600
 in the kernel you wrote in binary some new values.

143
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:13,160
 These are the ports that I want to look at

144
00:09:13,160 --> 00:09:15,560
 and the driver would capture those into a circular buffer

145
00:09:15,560 --> 00:09:21,960
 and you'd read kernel memory to pull that buffer out.

146
00:09:21,960 --> 00:09:27,200
 Craig Laris and Chris Torak who were working in my group

147
00:09:27,200 --> 00:09:35,440
 at LBL and were both long-time kernel hackers were just

148
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:39,760
 embarrassed at this and they put together a really nice clean

149
00:09:39,760 --> 00:09:44,000
 driver thing called BPF, the Berkeley Packet Filter

150
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:46,880
 that would let you pull packets out of the kernel

151
00:09:46,880 --> 00:09:51,160
 by a very efficient I/O control interface.

152
00:09:51,160 --> 00:10:00,640
 And so we bundled all of that up and on the TCP/IP mailing list

153
00:10:00,640 --> 00:10:05,800
 which in those days was, TCP/IP was very experimental,

154
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:08,600
 it was very leading edge and pretty much everybody

155
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:10,760
 who was playing with it was on that mailing list

156
00:10:10,760 --> 00:10:13,320
 to announce that this stuff was available.

157
00:10:13,320 --> 00:10:20,160
 A bunch of people FTPed it, tried it, it blew up,

158
00:10:20,160 --> 00:10:22,040
 sent kernel core dumps and bug reports

159
00:10:22,040 --> 00:10:27,440
 and fixed the bug reports and put new versions out.

160
00:10:27,440 --> 00:10:30,240
 Somebody would immediately come back and say, panicked here,

161
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:32,040
 do you want the K-Core?

162
00:10:32,040 --> 00:10:37,120
 And they'd go, oh no, embarrassed, put out a new version,

163
00:10:37,120 --> 00:10:40,400
 go out, somebody else would come back and say panicked here

164
00:10:40,400 --> 00:10:44,520
 and fix that and just cycled like that and after about a day

165
00:10:44,520 --> 00:10:46,520
 we got a version that didn't immediately panic

166
00:10:46,520 --> 00:10:51,560
 and then started working on the actual algorithms

167
00:10:51,560 --> 00:10:53,760
 and a little bit of tuning to make sure

168
00:10:53,760 --> 00:10:59,080
 that it actually did good all the time and didn't do any harm.

169
00:10:59,080 --> 00:11:02,440
 Just completely a community effort

170
00:11:02,440 --> 00:11:09,320
 and sort of when the community was saying,

171
00:11:09,320 --> 00:11:15,280
 this mostly does good and never seems to do harm,

172
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:20,040
 that's pretty much what Mike needed to put it into the kernel

173
00:11:20,040 --> 00:11:23,280
 so he took the community-developed modules

174
00:11:23,280 --> 00:11:25,080
 and rolled them into the BSD release.

